{
  "dom_id": "step-q160961",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "The region $R$ is bounded between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x.$ Given its representation \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{f}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {f}_{2}(x,z)}\n$$\n\n \nas a type III region in three-dimensional space, which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}$\n- $D={(x,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 2}$\n- ${f}_{1}(x,z)=2x$ and ${f}_{2}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "A typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063175/assets/c3333ded3e00e7d7408f0dbd37807f1adec0301691cc6311871bfea6d7b9fa15.png)\n\n \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. First, we find the projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $y={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$ and the plane $y=2x$ onto $xz$-plane.\n\nNotice that the equation of the plane is already solved for $y.$ Substituting this expression for $y$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={x}^{2}+{z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x+1)+{z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSo, the projection of the intersection is the circle $(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ centered at $(1,0)$ in the $xz$-plane. Hence, the domain $D$ is given by the following disc:\n\n$$\nD={(x,z)\\,:\\,(x-1)^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have that $y$ is\n\n- bounded *below* by ${f}_{1}(x,z)={x}^{2}+{z}^{2}$, and\n\n- bounded *above* by ${f}_{2}(x,z)=2x.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
